{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And on the day.</b> To include all other newborns who are circumcised, that they may not be circumcised except by day. <b>And on the eighth day he shall be circumcised.</b> The students of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai asked: why did the Torah say on the eighth? He said to them: so that not everyone be joyful — for the day of circumcision is a day of rejoicing, as it is written: ״שש אנכי על אמרתך״, and His word — that is circumcision. And the father and mother of the infant are distressed, for they are forbidden to each other; but on the eighth they are permitted, for she has already immersed on the evening of the seventh, and so everyone is joyful." "<b>And on the eighth day he shall be circumcised.</b> This commandment is repeated to teach: even on Shabbat. And what do I fulfill from ״מחלליה מות יומת״? — for all other preparatory labors of circumcision, which are prohibited, except for the circumcision itself." "<b>He shall be circumcised the flesh of his foreskin.</b> A definite foreskin overrides Shabbat, but a doubtful one does not override Shabbat, and twilight does not override Shabbat. And thus we learned: an infant may be circumcised at eight, at nine, at ten, at eleven, at twelve — no fewer and no more. How so? In the normal manner — at eight. At nine — how so? When he is born at twilight, for the eighth day of the morrow he is circumcised; but perhaps twilight is day, and so he is circumcised at nine. At ten — how so? When he is born at twilight of erev Shabbat, it is impossible to circumcise him on the coming Shabbat, for perhaps it is the ninth, and that would be a circumcision not in its proper time, which does not override Shabbat; and one must wait until after Shabbat, which is the tenth. At eleven — how so? When he is born at twilight of erev Shabbat and a festival falls immediately after the second Shabbat after his birth — a circumcision not in its proper time does not override it, and he is circumcised at eleven. At twelve — how so? When he is born at twilight between the exit of the twenty-second of Elul and the entrance of the twenty-third of Elul, which falls on Thursday, and the coming Thursday is the first day of Rosh HaShanah — it is impossible to circumcise him, for perhaps he is only at the ninth; and not on the morrow, the sixth of the week, for it is Shabbat, since both of them are one continuous sanctity; and not on the morrow of that, which is Shabbat — and one must wait until after Shabbat, which is the twelfth. Yaakov of Nevoraia ruled in Tzor: it is permitted to circumcise the son of a Tzorian woman on Shabbat. Rav Hagai heard; he said to him: you deserve lashes! He said to him: an ordinary person ruled a matter of Torah, and lashes? He said to him: and what is the Torah source? He said to him: as it is written, ״ויתילדו על משפחותם לבית אבותם״. He said to him: you have not ruled well, as it is written, ״לא תתחתן בם כי יסיר את בנך מאחרי״ — תסיר was not said, but rather יסיר — meaning, your Tzorian son-in-law will remove his son, who is your son, from following Me. This teaches: your son who comes from this Tzorian is called your son, but your son who comes from the Tzorian woman is not called your son, but rather her son. He said to him: ״רצוף ריצפך״ — they should thrash you with a packed-clay thrashing, for you are fit for instruction."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:11:45.431943518Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}